WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.920
Another mystery of Star Wars: The Rise of
Skywalker has been solved.

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:05.900
"Sabine — another one coming out!"

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:11.000
Near the film's end, right after the triumph
of Rey over granddad Emperor Palpatine, she

00:00:11.009 --> 00:00:15.190
and Kylo Ren appear to exchange words before
the latter vanishes into the Force.

00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:17.320
But no dialogue between the two is audible.

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:21.840
As it turns out, they did have some parting
words after all and the movie's novelization

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:24.800
is apparently letting us in on what they had
to say to each other.

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:29.050
You'll recall that Rey, egged on by the voices
of Jedi past in her head, was able to seal

00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:33.880
Palpatine's destruction by deflecting his
own hateful, nasty Force lightning back at

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:36.810
him, apparently mortally wounding herself
in the process.

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:40.960
Kylo, however having decided to go ahead and
turn back to the light side and become Ben

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:44.130
Solo again transferred his life force to her
at the last minute.

00:00:44.130 --> 00:00:47.880
They had those mysterious words, shared a
chaste kiss, and then he was gone.

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:49.060
"Curious."

00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:54.780
According to the novelization, their exchange
wasn't exactly the most creative thing in

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:55.280
the world.

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:58.660
If you happened to take a guess as to what
they might've said, the chances are fairly

00:00:58.660 --> 00:00:59.800
good that you were right.

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:01.580
"I couldn't really guess."

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:03.680
The relevant passage in the book reads,

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:06.550
"A voice came to her through the Force, clear
and strong.

00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:08.650
'I will always be with you,' Ben said.

00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:09.380
She smiled.

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:10.940
Let the truth of it wash over her.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:12.960
'No one's ever really gone,' she whispered."

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:15.340
"No one's ever really gone."

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:18.460
There you have it, folks; that clears that
right up.

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:23.120
The revelation is a fairly minor one among
the other story-related things that have become

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:25.940
known in the months following Rise of Skywalker's
release.

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:30.090
As is becoming increasingly clear, a whole
lot of seemingly important plot material for

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:32.940
Episode IX just straight-up wasn't in the
movie.

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:37.619
To understand what happened, Disney apparently
would rather you find out in a book sold separately,

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:38.140
of course.

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:39.780
"The sacred Jedi texts!"

00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:45.820
The Rise of Skywalker has been accompanied
by a pretty substantial amount of game-changing

00:01:45.830 --> 00:01:47.030
supplemental material.

00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:51.799
For starters, there's the movie's Visual Dictionary,
released on the same day the film came out;

00:01:51.799 --> 00:01:56.740
that ginormous tome revealed such plot points
as the fact that, as many suspected, ex-Stormtrooper

00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:59.360
Jannah is Lando Calrissian's long-lost daughter.

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:02.660
"The character you're playing, Jannah, she's
Lando Calrissian's daughter."

00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:03.700
"Yeah, there's that rumor."

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:08.440
That book also revealed that the planet Kylo
visits in the film's opening is indeed the

00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:13.700
same one Mustafar where Anakin Skywalker lost
his famous game of "The floor is lava" with

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:18.030
Obi-Wan Kenobi, and that the candy apple Sith
Troopers who show up out of nowhere to serve

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:22.400
on his Star Destroyers are actually the children
of Palpatine's worshippers on Exegol.

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:27.060
Oh, and for some reason it also suggests that
the vaunted Knights of the Old Republic RPG

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:28.460
is apparently canon again.

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:29.360
Why not?

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:32.680
"You cannot hide from what you once were,
Revan."

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:36.440
More lost plot points from the movie will
likely be laid out by the five-issue Rise

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:41.180
of Skywalker comic series, to be published
by Marvel Comics starting in May 2020.

00:02:41.510 --> 00:02:45.480
And you might recall that even more details
about the movie literally had to be explained

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:47.560
by the movie's crew in various interviews.

00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:52.120
For example, the film's editor Maryann Brandon
divulged in a conversation with Huffington

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:57.440
Post why Rey was unable to simply kill Emperor
Palpatine, explaining that she basically can't

00:02:57.440 --> 00:02:58.580
strike him down in anger.

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:02.680
Apparently, it's only by reflecting his own
hate back at him or in this case, lightning

00:03:02.690 --> 00:03:05.680
bolts that she's able to beat him at his nefarious
schemes.

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:10.060
On another occasion, director J.J. Abrams
himself revealed what it was that Finn kept

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:12.000
trying to tell Rey for the entire movie.

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:16.740
Turns out he wants to tell her that he's Force-sensitive,
and not, like you might imagine, that he secretly

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:17.340
loves her.

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:18.840
"You got a boyfriend?

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:19.340
Boyfriend?"

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:20.760
"It’s None of your business"

00:03:21.460 --> 00:03:25.840
Depending on whom you ask, this all makes
Rise of Skywalker either A) a thematically

00:03:25.849 --> 00:03:30.620
rich, narratively dense work containing too
much story to fit into its run time; or B)

00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:35.140
an incomplete film which asks its fans to
shell out extra cash to fill in its plot holes.

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:37.280
"Yeah, I think you're right."

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:41.720
If you happened to enjoy The Rise of Skywalker
just fine as-is, more power to you.

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:45.470
But either way, we hope the next movie can
manage to fit a whole story in before the

00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:48.120
credits roll, and not leave the good stuff
for the books.

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:54.620
The Rise of Skywalker will hit digital platforms
on March 17, and DVD/Blu-ray on March 31.

